Paths
description: the colon grammar - one meaning for the colon everywhere
- >
  Path is an expression, that allows to find certain node in Yamlover tree by starting from some node
  and the following the relations in the directed graph

- >
  The colon is THE key marker of the JSON/YAML family: `key: value` means "this key leads to that value", and
  alos it is a ptah separator

- >
    The path consists of segments, separated by colons. In the beginning ot the path there can be zero, one, two
    or three colons.
- >
    Example of paths:
- >
    Path shows how to walk inside the yamlover tree. For example, consider this tree:
- !!yo
    humans:
      - name: Alice
        pets:
          - name: Whiskers
            species: cat
          - name: Bubbles
            species: fish

- >
    The path to the name `Bubbles` inside this tree will be

- !!<format: text/plain> |
  humans: 0: pets: 1: name

- >
   which would mean

- !!<*:: yamlover: $defs: bullets>
  - enter into the child node keyed `humans`
  - then enter into 0th child node
  - then enter into the child node keyed `pets`
  - then enter 1th child node
  - then enter into the child node keyed `name`

- Segment can be

- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  - - Known token
    - `~`, `null`, `..`, `...`, `<<`, `-`
    - Works according to it's meaning
  - - Unquoted key
    - `abc`, `cde`
    - Leads to child node, connected by this key
  - - Integer
    - `0`, `1`, `100`
    - Leads to child of this ordinal number
  - - Quoted string
    - `"abc"`, `'cde'`, `"12"`
    - Leads to child node, connected by this key

- The beginning ot the path can be

- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  - - (none)
    - ~
    - Starting location is the current node
  - - Single colon
    - `:`
    - Starting location is document boundary
  - - Double colon
    - `::`
    - Starting location is project root
  - - Triple colon
    - `:::`
    - Starting location is world, global Internet or Registry




- >
  A path is a sequence of **portions**, typed by the **bare-token rule**: a portion of pure digits IS
  the integer key - a position (`: some: path: 12: with: ordinal`); a bare `~` is the null key;
  anything else bare, and any *quoted* portion, is a string key - `: '1'` is the numeric string, `:
  '~'` the literal tilde. The retired bracket index `[n]` reads forever as an alias, written never. A
  key containing a space must be quoted (the host surface's string quoting), as must a key whose bare
  form would read as something else - empty, pure digits, `~`, `-`+digits; a literal metacharacter is
  backslash-escaped ([escaping](::language:pointers:escaping)). The slash is an ordinary key
  character: `text/html` rides bare, and `*/pets` is a (dangling) reference to the literal key
  "/pets", never a rooted path.
- !!<format: text/plain> |
  deref    = "*" pointer          ; dereference - a graph edge (pull)
  define   = "&" pointer [ "[]" ] ; PATH anchor - also at that path
  pointer  = scope *( ":" portion )
  scope    = ":::" ws authority   ; the world
           / "::"                 ; current project root
           / ":"                  ; current document root
           / ".."                 ; parent node
           / portion              ; a key / position in the current mapping
  portion  = position             ; bare digits - the integer key n
           / nullkey              ; the null key
           / ( name / ".." ) *( relindex )
           / 1*( relindex )       ; a bare relative position: *[.-1]
  position = 1*DIGIT              ; : pets: 1  ([n] reads as an alias)
  nullkey  = "~"                  ; : doc: ~   ('~' is the literal key)
  relindex = "[" "." [("+"/"-") 1*DIGIT] "]"  ; host-relative
  name     = 1*( nchar / "\" CHAR ) / quoted
  nchar    = <any char except unescaped
              : [ ] * & # ~ ? ! ( ) < > = |  or whitespace>
- >
  The static **arity rule** classifies every expression by its form alone. If every portion guarantees
  at most one successor, the expression is a **link** - legal after `*` (an edge) and after `&` (an
  anchor). If any portion may fan out - a wildcard, a matcher, a descent, a fan-out uplink - it is a
  **query**: askable through the engine, never authorable as an edge; `something: *: team: ?` is a
  parse-time error. The classification is syntax-only today and designed to grow schema-aware - a
  schema proving "exactly one parent holds this relation" could admit an uplink into links - without
  moving the decision point.
